---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");

const crates = [
  ["corvene", "The binary: window, native menus, keymap, single-instance lock, the x-corvene:// links."],
  ["corvene-ui", "Every view, dialog, foldout and menu, built with GPUI. Never touches git, the network or disk."],
  ["corvene-core", "The dispatcher and application state: actions come in, git jobs run, state updates go out. The flags registry."],
  ["corvene-git", "Repository reads with gitoxide and every git subprocess, through one entry point with tracing and cancellation."],
  ["corvene-github", "The GitHub API: accounts, pull requests, checks, notifications."],
  ["corvene-store", "Settings, the repository list and the per-repository state in a redb database."],
  ["corvene-highlight", "Syntax highlighting: GitHub Desktop's modes, plus tree-sitter behind a flag."],
  ["corvene-grammars and corvene-packs", "The tree-sitter grammars and the on-demand packs that deliver them."],
  ["corvene-platform", "Keychain, Secret Service and Credential Manager, editors and shells, desktop entries, the Android bridge."],
  ["corvene-askpass", "The helper git calls for credentials, the same role GitHub Desktop's askpass trampoline plays."],
];
---

<DocsLayout
  currentSlug="architecture"
  title="Architecture"
  description="A Cargo workspace of focused crates: GPUI for rendering, gitoxide for reads, the git command line for writes, redb for state."
>
  <h2 id="crates">The workspace</h2>
  <p>
    Corvene is a workspace of crates with one direction of dependencies: the UI dispatches actions on
    <code>corvene_core::Dispatcher</code>, the core runs them against <code>corvene-git</code> and the platform crates, and
    the resulting state flows back into the views. UI crates never touch git, the network or disk.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Crate</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {crates.map(([name, text]) => (
        <tr>
          <td><code>{name}</code></td>
          <td>{text}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="git">Two ways of talking to git</h2>
  <h3>Reads in process, with gitoxide</h3>
  <p>
    Status, the commit log, diffs, blobs and refs come from <a href="https://github.com/GitoxideLabs/gitoxide" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">gitoxide</a>
    in the app's own process. There is no process to spawn and no output to parse, which is what makes selecting a file
    or scrolling History cheap, and it lets results be cached by object id so a refresh that changed nothing draws nothing.
  </p>
  <h3>Writes through your git</h3>
  <p>
    Anything that changes the repository (commit, checkout, merge, rebase, stash, push, fetch) runs the
    <code>git</code> on your <code>PATH</code>, through a single entry point that sets the environment, records a tracing
    span and supports cancellation. Hooks, credential helpers, signing, LFS and every setting in your
    <code>.gitconfig</code> therefore behave exactly as they do in GitHub Desktop, which does the same.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gpui">Rendering with GPUI</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://gpui.rs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GPUI</a> is the UI framework the Zed editor is built
    on. Views describe a tree of elements each frame; GPUI lays it out and draws it with Metal on macOS, Vulkan on Linux
    and Android, and DirectX on Windows. There is no DOM and no browser between the app and the screen. Long lists (the
    diff, History, the branch list) are virtualized, and expensive views are cached between frames.
  </p>
  <p>
    GitHub Desktop's layout constants, Primer colour tokens and both themes are reproduced from its source, which is what
    the <a href={`${base}/docs/parity`}>parity harness</a> checks.
  </p>

  <h2 id="state">State and settings</h2>
  <p>
    Settings, the repository list, per-repository state (last branch, commit message drafts) and the flags live in a
    <a href="https://github.com/cberner/redb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">redb</a> database in the platform's
    data directory. Account tokens go to the operating system's keychain, never to disk in the clear.
  </p>

  <h2 id="highlighting">Syntax highlighting</h2>
  <p>
    The default highlighter covers the languages GitHub Desktop's CodeMirror modes cover, so diffs look the same. Behind
    flag <code>105-tree-sitter-highlighting</code>, tree-sitter grammars take over for the 300+ languages it does not
    know (or for everything). The grammars are an optional download, one pack per OS and architecture, unpacked lazily
    the first time a language appears; the Full edition compiles them all in instead.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
